## Appendix A Results for additional \\chiEFT bands and astrophysical constraints

In addition to the results for the N{}^{3}LO \\chiEFT band with the "Baseline" and "New" scenarios discussed in the main text, we provide in the appendix results for the Keller et al. (2023) N{}^{2}LO and the Hebeler et al. (2013) \\chiEFT band, as well as for two other data scenarios that differ compared to "New" only in the J0030 results from Vinciguerra et al. (2024): "New 2" is based on ST+PST results for NICER data only, while "New 3" is based on PDT-U with a joint analysis of NICER and XMM data. The astrophysical results are summarized in Table 2 and shown as mass-radius regions in Fig. 11. All scenarios include the GW results.

### Posterior results for the "New" scenario and the Hebeler et al. and N{}^{2}LO \\chiEFT bands

In Fig. 12, we show the mass-radius posteriors for the "New" scenario, comparing the previously used Hebeler et al. (2013) band and the Keller et al. (2023) \\chiEFT bands at N{}^{2}LO and N{}^{3}LO with transition densities of 1.1n_{0} and 1.5n_{0}. For the transition density of 1.1n_{0}, the top panels of Fig. 12 show that the mass-radius posteriors of Hebeler et al. (2013) and N{}^{2}LO predict similar credible regions to the N{}^{3}LO band with only small differences in radii below 1.6\\,M_{\\odot}. For the transition density of 1.5n_{0}, the N{}^{3}LO \\chiEFT band is also very consistent with the N{}^{2}LO band, but at N{}^{2}LO we observe a broadening and a hint of a bimodal-like structure more pronounced at N{}^{2}LO than for the N{}^{3}LO band discussed in the main text. Similar to our results for the N{}^{3}LO \\chiEFT band, the bimodal-like structure also manifests in the posterior distributions of the polytropic fit parameter, K, for the N{}^{2}LO band as well as for the pressure at intermediate densities of 2n_{0} and 3n_{0}, as shown in Fig. 13.

As discussed in Sec. 4.2, a key quantity for the dense matter EOS is the maximum mass M_{\\rm TOV}. In Fig. 14, we give the joint posterior distributions of M_{\\rm TOV} and \\Delta R for the "New" scenario using the N{}^{2}LO \\chi EFT band up to 1.1n_{0} and 1.5n_{0}, which are overlaid with the corresponding N{}^{3}LO posteriors. This shows that the maximum mass is predicted to be below around 2.4\\,M_{\\odot} (95% CI) for all \\chiEFT assumptions, with slightly larger values for the CS models. Moreover, we observe that the posteriors on M_{\\rm TOV}-\\Delta R are largely unaffected when going from N{}^{2}LO to N{}^{3}LO.

### Sensitivities to J0030 NICER mass-radius results

We next explore the sensitivities to the J0030 NICER mass-radius results by comparing the "New 2" and "New 3" scenarios to the "Baseline" and "New" scenarios studied in the main text. To this end, we compare in Fig. 15 the mass-radius posterior distributions using the N{}^{3}LO \\chiEFT band up to 1.5n_{0} for the new scenarios to the "Baseline". Similar to what we found for "New," the "New 2" mass-radius posteriors shift to somewhat lower radii for the PP model, while for the CS model the posteriors narrow. This shifting/narrowing of the "New 2" posteriors is due to the combined effect of the J0030 and J0437 NICER results. For the "New 3" scenario, the posterior distributions narrow slightly compared to the "Baseline" scenario and both the PP and CS models fully remain within the "Baseline" contours. In this case, the J0030 PDT-U results, centered around R=14.44\\,km, compensate for the J0437 result that pushes the posteriors to lower radii, thus yielding mass-radius posteriors that favor neutron stars closer to 12 km compared to the "New" and "New 2" scenarios. Depending on the choice of whether to include background constraints for J0030 (and the preferred mode once those constraints are included), the mass-radius posteriors (and, by extension the EOS posteriors) are either--when compared to the "Baseline"--marginally unaffected ("New 2"), pushed toward lower masses and radii ("New"), or tightened at radii around 12 km ("New 3"). This variation in the inferred neutron star mass-radius relation highlights the dependence of our inferences on the choice of mass-radius posteriors of J0030.

